Enhancement: Recognise an unchanged wide directory without recompressing its tree

When restic saves a blob it hashes it, checks whether the repository already has
that blob, and only compresses and stores it if not. The streaming path added for
very wide directories could not do that, because it needed to compress in order to
hash, so the tree of an unchanged wide directory was compressed again on every
backup even though it was then discarded.

Such a tree is now hashed first and compressed only if the repository does not
already have it, which is the order used for every other blob. Recognising a tree
the repository already holds is between two and six times cheaper, the larger
difference being for trees whose content ids make them compress poorly, which is
what a real directory of files looks like.

